diff --git a/native-runtime/window/README.md b/native-runtime/window/README.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/native-runtime/window/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+# Native window observation component
+
+From the source directory, build independently with `cmake -S native-runtime/window -B <build>`,
+`cmake --build <build> --config Release --parallel 2`, then CTest. MSVC builds
+both the component and test with `/W4 /WX /analyze`. Link `native_window` (User32)
+into a caller explicitly; parent build and boot wiring are intentionally separate.
+
+`native::Window` creates a normal visible owned window only on explicit construction.
+All calls/destruction require its creating thread; there is no spawned thread,
+guest memory, service state or GPU dependency. `source_id()` is an instance generation
+independent of HWND reuse. `process_id()` is the actual owning host process ID, which
+the frontend must map explicitly to its guest process identity rather than confuse
+the two ID namespaces. Bind the instance before draining `Poll()` copies so genuine
+creation-time observations remain available. Initial focus is Unknown until an
+actual message/snapshot; constructor/show success alone is not focus proof.
+
+Focus means owned foreground-root equality from GetForegroundWindow. Null foreground
+means Unknown. Other foreground roots mean Inactive; this component assigns no
+Switch Background/suspend semantics. Pump also takes a real foreground snapshot so
+transient null observations can settle on subsequent pumping. Sources preserve the
+message or explicit foreground-poll origin. Records carry monotonic sequence,
+source generation, owned HWND, process ID, focus, visibility and minimized state.
+Only changed aggregate state is queued. HWND messages do not authenticate a human
+sender; tests clearly label programmatically posted/sent messages as synthetic.
+
+Call Pump with a bounded 1..1024 message count and drain Poll regularly. While parked,
+WaitAndPump accepts 0..50ms and uses MsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx with QS_ALLINPUT and
+MWMO_INPUTAVAILABLE, then pumps. A caller without a Win32 wake HANDLE gets bounded
+50ms polling for its other producers. Windows dispatch itself can invoke modal OS
+behavior; message count bounds do not guarantee a hard wall-clock deadline.
+
+A full fixed FIFO sets sticky overflowed(); the caller must fail lifecycle processing
+explicitly, never continue as though a complete stream arrived. The first close is
+retained separately even on overflow. WM_CLOSE/session shutdown request yields one
+close_requested record without destroying the window. WM_QUIT yields a distinct
+ThreadQuit record/getter with close_requested false; it is not a guest Exit request.
+Destroy disconnects observations, destroys on the owner and unregisters the unique
+class. Repeated Destroy is safe and does not emit close. Drain/cancel guest operations
+through their own owners before destruction; no guest lifetime is owned here.
+
+Official contracts checked:
+- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/creating-windows-in-threads
+- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-msgwaitformultipleobjectsex
+- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/winmsg/wm-activateapp
+- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-getforegroundwindow
+- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/learnwin32/closing-the-window
+
+Tests create real owned visible windows and record actual OS state without forcing
+foreground. They verify generation identity, class unregister, duplicate suppression,
+wrong-thread rejection, synthetic close delivered while idle, duplicate close,
+explicit real visibility-change overflow retaining close, and thread-quit distinction.
+They do not claim user activation, guest wait wakeup, lifecycle IPC or rendering proof.
